WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.323
(soft banjo music)

00:00:02.323 --> 00:00:06.949
(explosion)
(honky-tonk music)

00:00:06.949 --> 00:00:09.545
- I think they're going to use
a New York accent the most.

00:00:09.545 --> 00:00:10.877
- Southern.
- Some Southern.

00:00:10.877 --> 00:00:12.572
- Classic southern California valley girl.

00:00:12.572 --> 00:00:13.954
- Maybe a New Yorker.

00:00:13.954 --> 00:00:15.938
- Last time I went out with Brad,

00:00:15.938 --> 00:00:18.641
he spilled Faygo and Better Made chips

00:00:18.641 --> 00:00:20.260
all over me at the Tiger's game.

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:21.254
- He was trying to do Midwest,

00:00:21.254 --> 00:00:22.330
but that's totally valley girl.

00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:24.233
- That's how people talk in Michigan.

00:00:24.233 --> 00:00:25.275
I'm from Michigan.

00:00:25.275 --> 00:00:27.367
- You can do a Midwestern,

00:00:27.367 --> 00:00:29.647
you got to say mom, dad, and pop.

00:00:29.647 --> 00:00:31.331
- We're going to the Tiger's game.

00:00:31.331 --> 00:00:33.623
- Minnesota, you know all
those people up there--

00:00:33.623 --> 00:00:34.966
- Midwest, pretty good.

00:00:34.966 --> 00:00:37.149
- That's good, Santiago's good.

00:00:37.149 --> 00:00:39.900
- Why are they all doing
the Midwest accent?

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:41.031
- Santiago thinks we're Canadian,

00:00:41.031 --> 00:00:42.340
And I'm not okay with it.

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:43.478
- Where would they hear that?

00:00:43.478 --> 00:00:44.720
- Oh Sarah Palin.

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:46.416
- Oh Sarah Palin.
- Yeah.

00:00:46.416 --> 00:00:49.116
- I'm from America, and I
say words like aluminum,

00:00:49.116 --> 00:00:50.830
and spell color without the u.

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:51.988
- Did he say Americorps?

00:00:51.988 --> 00:00:54.913
- Isn't that a non-profit organization?

00:00:54.913 --> 00:00:55.892
- Where it's like aluminum?

00:00:55.892 --> 00:00:57.990
- Yeah, they say aluminium.

00:00:57.990 --> 00:00:59.400
- Crazy.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:01.386
- I would like a Duncan donut.

00:01:01.386 --> 00:01:04.542
- "I would like a Duncan donut."

00:01:04.542 --> 00:01:05.921
It sounded like Irish.

00:01:05.921 --> 00:01:08.482
- The sentences they're using,
- They think (spoken over).

00:01:08.482 --> 00:01:10.542
- They're all about food.

00:01:10.542 --> 00:01:12.430
- But I've said that to my mom, (laughter)

00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:14.840
multiple times, throughout my life.

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:16.127
- You know he looked at me and said,

00:01:16.127 --> 00:01:17.368
"You (beep) my wife."

00:01:17.368 --> 00:01:18.283
- [Voiceover] He's good.

00:01:18.283 --> 00:01:19.081
- Okay, he's pretty good.

00:01:19.081 --> 00:01:19.941
- You could tell which TV shows

00:01:19.941 --> 00:01:21.784
get syndicated abroad.

00:01:21.784 --> 00:01:22.593
- The Godfather.

00:01:22.593 --> 00:01:24.099
- These must come from the Sopranos.

00:01:24.099 --> 00:01:25.665
- Every mafia movie ever.

00:01:25.665 --> 00:01:28.218
- Why can't you be like, "I
want a pizza, go Knicks!"

00:01:28.218 --> 00:01:31.816
- So I say to myself,
"Jump on the horse, cowboy,

00:01:31.816 --> 00:01:33.010
"and do your best."

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:34.945
(laughter)

00:01:34.945 --> 00:01:37.627
- No, this is the funniest one.

00:01:37.627 --> 00:01:39.713
- That is like a New
Orleans kind of accent,

00:01:39.713 --> 00:01:42.403
"Cowboy, jump on that horse"

00:01:42.403 --> 00:01:45.080
- Your eyebrows are like, um, sleek.

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:46.276
- They get points, in my book,

00:01:46.276 --> 00:01:47.643
if they know these fawn-dah words.

00:01:47.643 --> 00:01:49.105
- People always touch their hair,

00:01:49.105 --> 00:01:50.402
when they do California accent.

00:01:50.402 --> 00:01:52.469
I think that's part of doing
the accent, they're like,

00:01:52.469 --> 00:01:53.761
"Oh my God."

00:01:53.761 --> 00:01:56.405
Well I do touch my hair
a lot though. (laughter)

00:01:56.405 --> 00:01:59.377
- Those small things, in our
lives that keep us rolling,

00:01:59.377 --> 00:02:00.840
that keep us going.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:02.442
- How do they know how
to do a Southern accent?

00:02:02.442 --> 00:02:05.438
- I love the things they say.

00:02:05.438 --> 00:02:08.061
- Yeah, Jack, you should
go into voice acting.

00:02:08.061 --> 00:02:10.189
- That's deep-cut and that's
what we were looking for.

00:02:10.189 --> 00:02:11.362
- I'm impressed overall.

00:02:11.362 --> 00:02:13.089
- They see America a
little bit more accurately

00:02:13.089 --> 00:02:14.133
than I thought.

00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:16.734
- They're hearing like
Seinfeld, and Friends.

00:02:16.734 --> 00:02:20.054
- It just makes me happy to see,

00:02:20.054 --> 00:02:21.714
what other people think of us.

00:02:21.714 --> 00:02:23.182
- Why can't we be like, "Hey ma'am,

00:02:23.182 --> 00:02:25.098
"I'd like to help you cross the street."

00:02:25.098 --> 00:02:27.164
- I think that they're a 100% better

00:02:27.164 --> 00:02:29.346
at doing American accents than we are,

00:02:29.346 --> 00:02:30.740
at doing foreign accents.

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:33.619
- I'll call my brother in Sao
Paulo, we grew up together.

00:02:33.619 --> 00:02:35.662
- It's summertime in this city,

00:02:35.662 --> 00:02:38.909
and where are these summer
girls in their outfits?

00:02:38.909 --> 00:02:41.405
- I'm a put some shrimp on the barbie,

00:02:41.405 --> 00:02:43.263
crikey it bit me finger.

00:02:43.263 --> 00:02:45.790
(whoosh and squeak)

